44
00:03:33,000 --> 00:03:39,000
How do we provide the right level of
support so learners can learn
independently?

45
00:03:39,366 --> 00:03:44,000
And how do we keep learners continuously
engaged?

46
00:03:44,000 --> 00:03:51,000
But today we are going to use the
perspective of viewing user experience as
a learning experience.

47
00:03:51,000 --> 00:03:52,601
So let's try

48
00:03:52,601 --> 00:03:56,000
to change the word
"learn" into "use"

49
00:03:57,000 --> 00:04:00,078
So how do we help?

50
00:04:00,595 --> 00:04:01,363
Can anyone guess?

51
00:04:02,875 --> 00:04:03,643
Users, thank you.

52
00:04:04,585 --> 00:04:05,115
Users.

53
00:04:12,733 --> 00:04:15,000
users persist instead of
giving up?

54
00:04:15,000 --> 00:04:19,700
And how do we provide the right level of
support so...

55
00:04:21,300 --> 00:04:25,000
Great, thank you.
Users can?

56
00:04:27,000 --> 00:04:31,000
Use! Amazing. Use independently.

57
00:04:31,000 --> 00:04:33,396
And the last one, how do we keep

58
00:04:34,233 --> 00:04:35,315
Perfect.

59
00:04:35,315 --> 00:04:39,000
How do we keep users
continuously engaged?

60
00:04:39,000 --> 00:04:43,456
And I hope you can now see what I'm trying
to tell you.

61
00:04:44,566 --> 00:04:48,000
So I'm going to introduce three learning
theories.

62
00:04:48,000 --> 00:04:51,000
The basic ones, I'm not going to explain
too much.

63
00:04:51,000 --> 00:04:55,000
I'll bring lots of examples and the
strategies as well.

64
00:04:55,000 --> 00:05:01,000
So I'm going to talk about the cognitive
load theory and then ZPD,

65
00:05:01,000 --> 00:05:05,000
Zone of Proximal Development and
Scaffolding.

66
00:05:05,000 --> 00:05:09,000
And the last one is the flow theory.

67
00:05:09,466 --> 00:05:13,000
So the first one, the cognitive load
theory.

68
00:05:13,000 --> 00:05:19,000
This was introduced by Dr. John Sweller
in 1988.

69
00:05:19,000 --> 00:05:23,000
And we all know about working memory.

70
00:05:23,000 --> 00:05:27,000
We all know that we should not exceed
working memory.

71
00:05:27,000 --> 00:05:33,000
So the core idea of cognitive load theory
is that when designing for learning,

72
00:05:33,000 --> 00:05:41,000
we should avoid exceeding the limits of
working memory so that learners can stay
focused on the task itself.

73
00:05:43,300 --> 00:05:46,533
This theory introduces three types of
load.

74
00:05:47,000 --> 00:05:52,649
Intrinsic load, extraneous load, and
germane load.

75
00:05:54,124 --> 00:06:00,000
The first is intrinsic load, which refers to
the inherent complexity of the task
itself.

76
00:06:01,980 --> 00:06:11,253
The second is extraneous load, which comes
from unnecessary or inefficient elements
that are not directly related to the task.

77
00:06:11,341 --> 00:06:13,000
So it distracts the learner.

78
00:06:14,830 --> 00:06:20,000
The last one, the germane load, it is the
effort required to build schemas.

79
00:06:20,000 --> 00:06:27,000
It's the mental structure that supports
learning, so it links the new information
to the current information.

80
00:06:30,978 --> 00:06:36,000
And we should minimize one thing, maximize
another, and manage the rest.

81
00:06:36,038 --> 00:06:40,038
So can you guess which one we should
minimize?

82
00:06:44,000 --> 00:06:46,000
All of them?

83
00:06:46,000 --> 00:06:52,000
If some things are inherent, we cannot
really deal with it, do anything with it.

84
00:06:52,042 --> 00:06:57,042
But we should minimize extraneous load.

85
00:06:58,599 --> 00:07:03,439
These are unnecessary mental effort caused
by poor instructional design

86
00:07:04,226 --> 00:07:07,271
or distracting presentation.

87
00:07:08,332 --> 00:07:12,142
So we should minimize it by removing irrevalant information,

88
00:07:12,147 --> 00:07:14,198
using clear and concise information

89
00:07:14,198 --> 00:07:19,704
and avoiding uncluttered layout or unnecessary animations.

90
00:07:27,997 --> 00:07:29,997
Now I feel safe.

91
00:07:30,000 --> 00:07:33,000
Can you guess which one we should
maximize?

92
00:07:34,000 --> 00:07:36,000
Yes.

93
00:07:36,000 --> 00:07:41,000
Some of you were pointing, and that's
correct.

94
00:07:41,000 --> 00:07:44,000
We should maximize the germane load.

95
00:07:44,000 --> 00:07:46,000
That is where learning happens.

96
00:07:46,000 --> 00:07:53,485
So this is a productive mental effort used
to actually process the information,

97
00:07:53,504 --> 00:07:56,000
build schemas, and commit to long-term memory.

98
00:07:56,997 --> 00:08:03,619
So we should maximize it by encouraging
deep thinking or self-reflection and

99
00:08:03,635 --> 00:08:07,805
helping learners connect new concepts to
their prior knowledge.

100
00:08:09,000 --> 00:08:17,000
And the last one, we should manage
intrinsic load.

101
00:08:18,000 --> 00:08:26,000
We should manage it because intrinsic load
is inherent difficulty of the task itself,
so there is not much we can do.

102
00:08:26,000 --> 00:08:30,794
But we can help them or we can guide them
through

103
00:08:30,871 --> 00:08:38,801
making them in smaller digestible
chunks and also sequencing it logically
from simple to complex.

104
00:08:41,000 --> 00:08:44,000
Now back to our app and the user.

105
00:08:44,000 --> 00:08:53,000
If we throw too much at a user, they
experience an extraneous load, they get
overwhelmed, and they might leave.

106
00:08:53,000 --> 00:08:57,000
So we need to minimize this so they can
focus on the core value.

107
00:08:59,000 --> 00:09:02,213
Now let's take a look at some examples

108
00:09:02,282 --> 00:09:07,000
of how these strategies for reducing
cognitive load can be applied into UX.

109
00:09:09,000 --> 00:09:11,000
Does anyone use Chrome here?

110
00:09:12,000 --> 00:09:15,000
Yay, thank you. I do too.

111
00:09:15,000 --> 00:09:22,000
And when you press Command plus P, whether
in Chrome or not, usually, can you guess
what happens?

112
00:09:24,000 --> 00:09:26,000
P? Print!

113
00:09:26,000 --> 00:09:28,000
Yeah, you're right. Thank you.

114
00:09:28,000 --> 00:09:29,000
Print!

115
00:09:29,000 --> 00:09:31,000
So print dialog pops up.

116
00:09:31,000 --> 00:09:36,000
But when this dialog pops up, it does not
show you 100 settings.

117
00:09:37,000 --> 00:09:46,000
It only asks its destination and pages,
like the range of pages, and the layout,
landscape or portrait.

118
00:09:46,000 --> 00:09:49,000
So it only asks three questions.

119
00:09:49,000 --> 00:09:58,000
But if you're a power user and if you want
more, of course you can simply click more
settings and then you can adjust a lot of
things.

120
00:10:02,300 --> 00:10:07,300
So at first, a system presents a
simplified view.

121
00:10:07,300 --> 00:10:18,708
If all of these detailed options were
presented to the user at the moment they
tried to do it the first time, it would
create a massive cognitive bottleneck.

122
00:10:18,708 --> 00:10:23,300
So instead, the system completely removes
that initial mental barrier.

123
00:10:24,701 --> 00:10:30,175
And Apple highlighted this exact concept
back in WWDC 2022.

124
00:10:30,175 --> 00:10:37,934
So if you're interested, you can check out
"The Craft of SwiftUI APIs: Progressive
Disclosure."

125
00:10:41,000 --> 00:10:43,000
Now let's take a look at Airbnb.

126
00:10:43,000 --> 00:10:51,733
So when I open the app and when I start
searching, it does not overwhelm me with
information.

127
00:10:51,733 --> 00:10:53,733
It only asks where.

128
00:10:56,352 --> 00:10:59,484
And then just when.

129
00:11:03,601 --> 00:11:05,601
And next, who.

130
00:11:09,526 --> 00:11:21,956
So by chunking the information, Airbnb
reduces cognitive load at the beginning,
allowing users to focus on the core task,
which is browsing and choosing a place to
stay.

131
00:11:24,552 --> 00:11:29,312
But actually, if the user next time opens
the app

132
00:11:29,312 --> 00:11:34,895
they are given a shortcut so they can focus on what matters most, which is browsing and choosing.

133
00:11:37,267 --> 00:11:44,714
It already has a shortcut of Taipei,
everything, and it automatically shows the
result right away.

134
00:11:47,300 --> 00:11:49,300
And another example is autofill.

135
00:11:49,696 --> 00:11:57,300
So this is something that we can go even
further and eliminate the behavioral load
entirely.

136
00:11:57,300 --> 00:12:07,300
When you receive a text message with
authentication code, we used to have to go
to the message and maybe memorize the
numbers and type in.

137
00:12:07,687 --> 00:12:19,741
But now, it automatically detects the code
and it will surface the numbers above the
keyboard, so you can just tap it and it
gets filled right away.

138
00:12:19,741 --> 00:12:22,148
So this is like zero cognitive friction.

139
00:12:27,300 --> 00:12:31,300
And let me introduce the CapWords.

140
00:12:31,300 --> 00:12:36,300
This is a 2025 Apple design award winner
for delight and fun.

141
00:12:36,300 --> 00:12:44,300
And with Apple's frameworks, it replaces
manual data input with more seamless
interactions.

142
00:12:45,797 --> 00:12:46,797
Let me show you.

143
00:13:33,965 --> 00:13:39,574
It has a sound, but unfortunately, we
can't hear it.

144
00:13:44,052 --> 00:13:45,566
I'll just move on.

145
00:13:48,300 --> 00:13:54,300
So let me summarize what you can apply.

146
00:13:54,300 --> 00:13:57,300
I'm going to give you what you can apply
to your apps.

147
00:13:57,300 --> 00:14:06,300
So first, you should limit distractions.
And then use clear language.

148
00:14:06,300 --> 00:14:08,142
Group related information.

149
00:14:09,213 --> 00:14:15,300
Avoid unnecessary details.
And emphasize important content.

150
00:14:15,300 --> 00:14:20,678
And beyond these, any approach that helps
reduce cognitive load is relevant.

151
00:14:21,300 --> 00:14:26,931
And I'm just giving you only five, but any
approach will do.

152
00:14:26,931 --> 00:14:30,707
Now the second theory, ZPD and
Scaffolding.

153
00:14:32,769 --> 00:14:34,769
The Zone of Proximal Development.

154
00:14:34,769 --> 00:14:41,300
It was introduced by Lev Vygotsky, and the
Scaffolding was developed by Jerome
Bruner.

155
00:14:41,300 --> 00:14:49,671
And these two concepts provide a clear
framework for when and how to intervene
when a learner is learning a new thing.

156
00:14:51,906 --> 00:14:54,906
There are three zones.

157
00:14:55,300 --> 00:15:02,712
Zone A is something a learner can do alone
without any help, so a learner has already
mastered it.

158
00:15:04,300 --> 00:15:09,300
Zone C is something a learner cannot do
even with the help.

159
00:15:09,300 --> 00:15:13,269
This is beyond their level, so they cannot
do even with the guidance.

160
00:15:15,667 --> 00:15:21,300
And Zone B is where a learner can do with
proper help.

161
00:15:21,300 --> 00:15:28,300
So this is where the learning happens, and
this is called the Zone of Proximal
Development.

162
00:15:28,300 --> 00:15:35,080
And this is the sweet spot that we need to
focus and provide proper guidance and
help.

163
00:15:38,023 --> 00:15:42,024
Can anyone guess what these structures are
outside of the building?

164
00:15:42,607 --> 00:15:46,607
It's called scaffolding, yes.

165
00:15:46,607 --> 00:15:49,300
These are scaffolding.

166
00:15:49,300 --> 00:15:56,943
So you use scaffoldings when you're
constructing so that workers can go higher and
build a building.

167
00:15:56,943 --> 00:16:03,300
And after a building can stand on its own,
after it's stable, then you take these
scaffoldings away.

168
00:16:03,300 --> 00:16:11,555
So like this, we should provide proper
help and guidance, but as the learner
progresses, we should take them away.

169
00:16:13,300 --> 00:16:20,300
So at first we provide full support, and
then we slightly decrease the assistance.

170
00:16:20,300 --> 00:16:28,300
And then the final goal will be for the
users to do things independently.

171
00:16:28,300 --> 00:16:33,300
Let's take a look at some examples.

172
00:16:33,300 --> 00:16:35,300
Swift Playground.

173
00:16:35,300 --> 00:16:39,300
This helps through a series of dynamic
scaffolds.

174
00:16:39,300 --> 00:16:47,300
The app provides glossary popovers and
code completion bars that suggest the
exact syntax needed at first.

175
00:16:47,300 --> 00:16:55,300
But as the user progresses to later
chapters, the hints become less
prescriptive and more conceptual.

176
00:16:55,300 --> 00:17:04,060
So eventually the guided completion bar is
replaced by a standard keyboard, removing
the entire scaffolding.

177
00:17:07,300 --> 00:17:14,300
Tips from Apple is also a great example of
scaffolding and also reducing cognitive
load.

178
00:17:14,300 --> 00:17:24,300
Instead of overwhelming the user with a
full manual or complex documentation, it
delivers small, digestible tips over time.

179
00:17:24,300 --> 00:17:35,300
So it introduces features gradually, often
through short, focused pieces of guidance
or occasional notification.

180
00:17:35,300 --> 00:17:45,300
And as users become familiar, that support
fades away. And if the user uses
shortcuts, then they do not give you any
more tips.

181
00:17:45,300 --> 00:17:58,300
So it appears when you are near the skill
but haven't mastered it yet. And when the
user has mastered it, then it stops.

182
00:17:58,300 --> 00:18:01,300
The scaffolding doesn't have to be a text
tooltip.

183
00:18:01,300 --> 00:18:14,300
So if you look at the Goodnotes, when a
user draws a crooked shape, then algorithm
detects it and makes it into a perfect
shape.

184
00:18:14,300 --> 00:18:26,300
It acts as an invisible scaffold for
drawing beginners, and it makes them feel
capable.

185
00:18:26,300 --> 00:18:38,300
Notion is actually a very interesting
example because it's a powerful and quite
complex tool, yet it works hard to reduce
that complexity for new users.

186
00:18:38,300 --> 00:18:43,300
So when you first start, Notion doesn't
overwhelm you with all the features.

187
00:18:43,300 --> 00:18:45,468
It simply tells you

188
00:18:45,468 --> 00:18:50,910
"Press space for AI or slash for commands".
And when you press slash, 

189
00:18:50,910 --> 00:18:59,482
Notion reveals a wide range of
features, and by just hovering on them, it
gives you the short descriptions or
examples.

190
00:19:01,300 --> 00:19:08,538
So Notion steps in lightweight guidance,
and over time, users remember the commands
and no longer need the prompts.

191
00:19:16,961 --> 00:19:25,300
To apply ZPD and scaffolding to UX, here
are a few principles to follow.

192
00:19:25,300 --> 00:19:35,091
And please remember, in practice, we don't
apply just one learning theory, and these
approaches work best when it's all
combined together.

193
00:19:36,863 --> 00:19:41,065
So it should detect users' hesitation.

194
00:19:41,065 --> 00:19:43,497
Give contextual hints.

195
00:19:44,566 --> 00:19:47,369
Offer temporary support.

196
00:19:48,375 --> 00:19:55,100
Fade assistance gradually. And
then empower independent action.

197
00:19:58,004 --> 00:20:02,342
The last one is a flow theory coined by
Mihaly Csikszentmihalyi.

198
00:20:02,342 --> 00:20:05,661
And I'm pretty
sure you all have heard of him.

199
00:20:06,771 --> 00:20:18,384
So the flow theory is, if you want your
user to be fully immersed, you have to
meticulously calibrate the difficulty of
their experience.

200
00:20:19,066 --> 00:20:28,791
So if your app is too challenging, then
users feel anxiety, and they feel worried,
frustrated, and they might leave. They might quit.

201
00:20:29,300 --> 00:20:33,300
But if it's too easy, they get bored, and
they quit.

202
00:20:33,300 --> 00:20:40,999
So you have to keep them right between
them so that users can still stay in the
flow state.

203
00:20:42,300 --> 00:20:49,128
This is all. Let's take a look at some
examples of how the strategies of flow can
be applied.

204
00:20:51,833 --> 00:20:54,654
Elevate is a brain training app.

205
00:20:54,654 --> 00:21:01,544
It is a strong example of how learning experiences
can be designed around flow theory.

206
00:21:02,708 --> 00:21:12,300
So as you play the games, the on-device
algorithm constantly analyzes the accuracy
and reaction speed in the background.

207
00:21:12,300 --> 00:21:21,239
So it dynamically calibrates the math or
language difficulty so that learners can
stay in the flow state.

208
00:21:25,300 --> 00:21:37,300
This can be understood through
ZPD, where app identifies the gap between
the user's current ability to potential
through data.

209
00:21:37,300 --> 00:21:42,800
So from a scaffolding perspective,

210
00:21:42,800 --> 00:21:48,300
the app learns user behavior and provides timely
hints for support when needed.

211
00:21:48,764 --> 00:21:57,300
And ultimately, the app behaves like a
teacher, so even allowing for gradual
removal of support over time.

212
00:21:59,714 --> 00:22:09,300
I'm pretty sure you all know Duolingo.
This is actually a great example of
educational app that goes beyond flow
theory.

213
00:22:10,300 --> 00:22:18,300
Duolingo applies so many strategies, and
it delivers personalized learning from the
data.

214
00:22:18,300 --> 00:22:22,182
And also, it suggests a new goal,

215
00:22:22,182 --> 00:22:29,058
and it uses a lot of gamification strategies like
rankings and badges and nudges and
everything

216
00:22:29,540 --> 00:22:33,300
to just keep learners stay
focused and engaged.

